-- title:
--   Étale local sections of smooth schemes over henselian DVRs
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a discrete valuation ring which is a henselian local ring (a domain with a discrete valuation, in the Mathlib sense), let $X$ be a scheme and let $f \colon X \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ be a smooth morphism. Let $V$ be an open subset of $X$ and $x$ a point of $X$ lying in $V$ whose image under the underlying map of $f$ is the closed point of $R$, i.e. $x$ lies in the special fibre. The assertion is that there exist a ring $R'$ which is a domain and a discrete valuation ring, an $R$-algebra structure on $R'$ making $R'$ a finite $R$-module, étale and faithfully flat over $R$, with $\operatorname{Spec}$-level locality in the sense that $R \to R'$ is a local ring homomorphism and $R'$ is again a henselian local ring, together with a morphism of schemes $a \colon \operatorname{Spec} R' \to X$, such that: the ideal of $R'$ generated by the image of the maximal ideal of $R$ is the maximal ideal of $R'$ (so the extension is unramified); $a$ followed by $f$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the structure map $R \to R'$, i.e. $a$ is a section of $X$ over $\operatorname{Spec} R'$; and the image under $a$ of the closed point of $R'$ lies in $V$.
--
--   This is the standard statement that a smooth scheme over a henselian discrete valuation ring acquires, after a finite unramified (étale local) extension of the base, a section passing through any prescribed open subset of the special fibre; classically it appears in the construction of Néron models. It is used in the treatment of group laws on good-reduction Jacobians and in the Néron model infrastructure.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Smooth_exists_finite_etale_dvr_specMap_comp_eq_apply_closedPoint_mem_of_henselianLocalRing.lean

